      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Despite the railroad’s inconvenient location, Meridian grew into a thriving shipping hub for surrounding farms in the late 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/meridian-tx",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s oldest active Baptist church, built in 1882, showcases a Gothic Revival sanctuary with lancet windows and a segmented glass arch.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historictexas.net/city/meridian-texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 2004, Meridian’s historic church was designated a Recorded Texas Historic Landmark, recognizing its architectural and cultural significance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historictexas.net/city/meridian-texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Meridian serves as the county seat of Bosque County, located forty‑seven miles northwest of Waco in central Texas.",
